Gastrointestinal bleeding
Acute Urgent Care
Haematemesis/Melaena, Postural hypotension, Tachycardia, Active bleeding on endoscopy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AUC-033
Reason for Visit: Vomiting dark blood and passing black, foul-smelling stools, accompanied by severe dizziness and racing heart.
Location: Nursing home
Present at Visit: Patient and facility nursing staff
Urgency Justification: The nursing facility reported a patient passing massive amounts of black, tarry stool (melaena) and vomiting coffee-ground material, resulting in the patient nearly fainting when attempting to stand. This presentation defines a life-threatening acute haemorrhage. Urgent, on-site assessment was mandated to evaluate the degree of hypovolaemic shock and to facilitate immediate emergency transfer for life-saving endoscopy and blood transfusions.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: [Nurse reports] He threw up dark brown fluid all over his lunch tray, and his last bowel movement was pure black and sticky. He says he feels like he is going to pass out.
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a rapid onset of upper gastrointestinal bleeding over the last 6 hours. The nursing staff report two episodes of haematemesis resembling coffee grounds. Crucially, the patient has passed three large melaena stools (black, tarry, and extremely foul-smelling) over the course of the morning. The patient complains of profound lightheadedness and presyncope upon attempting to stand or sit upright in bed. There is notable pallor, diaphoresis, and cold extremities. The patient has a history of severe osteoarthritis and takes daily high-dose non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs).
Functional Status: Bedbound due to profound weakness and postural hypotension.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of exsanguination; patient is frail.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 95/60 (Supine) | HR: 118 | Temp: 36.4 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 96% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Patient appears highly lethargic, profoundly pale, and diaphoretic, lying completely flat in bed.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals resting tachycardia and a weak, thready pulse. Capillary refill is delayed (4 seconds). Blood pressure is borderline low while supine; postural testing is contraindicated due to the high risk of severe syncope, but postural hypotension is clinically evident from the history. Abdominal exam reveals mild, diffuse epigastric tenderness without rigidity or guarding. Rectal examination confirms the presence of black, sticky melaena on the glove. The patient's mental status is slightly altered (sluggish responses), indicating early cerebral hypoperfusion. Expected investigations include an urgent upper endoscopy revealing an actively bleeding gastric or duodenal ulcer, alongside an acute, severe drop in serum haemoglobin.
Environmental Observations: Incontinence pad heavily soiled with melaena.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient is on regular Naproxen 500mg twice daily for osteoarthritis.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Gastrointestinal bleeding - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Dietary/Medication Discoloration (e.g., Iron supplements) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the concurrent presentation of haematemesis, profound tachycardia, and signs of hypovolaemic shock, which confirm true active bleeding.

Lower GI Bleed (e.g., Diverticular hemorrhage) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of coffee-ground haematemesis, which definitively localizes the bleeding source proximal to the ligament of Treitz (upper GI tract).

Gastroenteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the explicit presence of digested blood (melaena/coffee grounds) rather than watery diarrhea, and the absence of a high fever.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits the classic diagnostic signs of a massive upper gastrointestinal bleed: haematemesis, melaena, resting tachycardia, and profound symptomatic hypotension. The chronic use of NSAIDs makes a bleeding peptic ulcer the most probable etiology. The patient is entering Class III hemorrhagic shock. An urgent home assessment was absolutely critical to physically confirm the shock state and immediately trigger a Priority 1 ambulance response. Waiting for a routine facility doctor review the next day would have undoubtedly resulted in fatal exsanguination.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Instructed nursing staff to place the bed in the Trendelenburg position (head flat, legs elevated) to maximize cerebral perfusion. Kept patient strictly Nil By Mouth (NBM). Ceased all NSAID administration immediately. Called 000 for Priority 1 Intensive Care Paramedic dispatch for aggressive fluid resuscitation.
Positive Behavioral Change: (Focus in this acute state is purely stabilization; however, future advice entails completely ceasing all NSAID medications to allow the severe stomach ulcers to heal and prevent recurrent catastrophic bleeding.)
Home-Based Services (Optional): Immediate transport to a tertiary hospital with gastroenterology services.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handover to paramedics and ED team.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance dispatched. Nursing staff must monitor the patient continuously. If the patient begins to actively vomit bright red blood, immediately roll him into the lateral recovery position to prevent aspiration and suction the airway if necessary.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: You have severe internal bleeding in your stomach, likely from a bleeding ulcer caused by your arthritis pain medications.
Clinical Course: You require immediate hospital treatment. The doctors will give you fluids and potentially blood transfusions, and then use a camera (endoscope) to look inside your stomach and physically stop the bleeding.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not try to sit up or get out of bed, as your blood pressure is critically low and you will faint. Keep your head completely flat on the pillow.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: We have laid you flat and raised your legs to ensure whatever blood you have left goes straight to your brain to keep you conscious. We have stopped your arthritis tablets permanently, as they have eroded the lining of your stomach.
Actionable Steps: Try to take slow, deep breaths. Do not swallow any more blood if you feel it coming up; spit it out into the bowl the nurse is holding.

